{"nodes": [{"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_technical_report", "label": "CollabNet technical report", "file_type": "document", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Technical explanation of CollabNet: chat, user discovery, and real-time file transfer on raw UDP, with a custom reliable transport and AES-256-GCM, plus an HTTP and client-side JavaScript UI. Checked with 19 automated tests and a real Chromium session. Python 3.10+, raw sockets and asyncio, and cryptography as the only external library. Flask, Django, WebSocket, and QUIC or aioquic are not used."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_three_layer_architecture", "label": "Three-layer architecture", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Network logic (common/transport.py, client/core.py, server/server.py), data processing (protocol.py, crypto.py), and the UI (client/web, plain HTML and JavaScript) are separated. The browser speaks only HTTP to a local gateway. Every packet between machines is UDP."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_reliable_udp", "label": "Reliable UDP delivery", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Packet numbers increase monotonically, and a retransmission gets a new number. Acknowledgements are ranges (SACK). Loss is declared by a threshold of 3 packets, a time threshold of 9/8 RTT, and a probe timeout. This follows the course text chapter 3: reliable data transfer, Go-Back-N and selective repeat, and TCP RTT estimation."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_per_stream_ordering", "label": "Per-stream ordering", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Each message and each file is its own stream, so only bytes inside one stream must stay in order. A lost file packet therefore does not block chat, which is the HTTP/2 to HTTP/3 and QUIC idea from chapter 2. Chat is scheduled ahead of files, and files are round-robin. Measured chat delay is about 21 ms while a 3 MB file moves at 3 percent loss. A single TCP-style byte stream would have stalled the messages."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_flow_control", "label": "Per-stream flow control", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The receive window slides per stream through MAX_STREAM_DATA and is 512 KB. The server holds the sender's credit when the receiver is slow, which is back-pressure, matching chapter 3 flow control and rwnd."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_cubic_and_bbr", "label": "CUBIC and BBR with pacing", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "CUBIC is the default; BBR is selected with --cc bbr, and both are paced. On a 2 MB transfer at 20 ms simulated RTT with no loss, CUBIC reaches 51.76 Mbps and BBR 38.17 Mbps, because CUBIC slow-start is more aggressive. At 1, 3, and 5 percent loss CUBIC drops to 6.46, 2.78, and 2.43 Mbps because each loss multiplies the window by 0.7, while BBR stays near 37.33, 26.89, and 29.17 Mbps using a bandwidth and RTT model. Chapter 3 contrasts that loss-based and model-based control. The algorithm is a parameter for that reason. The shipped BBR is a reduced version 1."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_handshake_security", "label": "Handshake security", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "There is no stock TLS on this UDP transport, so standard primitives are composed. Ephemeral X25519 gives forward secrecy, HKDF derives two AES-256-GCM keys (one per direction), and the server signs the handshake with Ed25519 so the client can pin the fingerprint (TOFU) against a man-in-the-middle, as in slide 8-41 and an SSH-style pin. The 13-byte header is additional authenticated data. A replay window rejects reused packet numbers. The nonce is four zero bytes concatenated with the monotonic packet number, so it never repeats. Stream and credit limits bound memory. Chapter 8 covers Diffie-Hellman, signatures, man-in-the-middle, TLS 1.3, and the IPsec replay window."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_x25519", "label": "X25519", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_hkdf", "label": "HKDF", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_aes_256_gcm", "label": "AES-256-GCM", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_ed25519", "label": "Ed25519", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_tofu", "label": "TOFU fingerprint pin", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_presence", "label": "Beacon and PEERS presence", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The server broadcasts a UDP beacon. It keeps the peer list (user, IP, port, since) and pushes PEERS to everyone whenever membership changes. Chapters 2 and 6 are the cited presence and discovery material."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_datagram", "label": "1200-byte datagram", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Layout is type (1 byte), destination connection id (4 bytes), packet number (8 bytes), encrypted frames up to 1171 bytes, and a 16-byte GCM tag. The 13-byte header is authenticated as additional data. Datagrams stay at or below 1200 bytes, under the MTU, and payload is cut into STREAM frames. That avoids IP fragmentation, which would drop the whole datagram if one fragment were lost."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_initial", "label": "INITIAL packet 0x01", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Cleartext client hello: 4-byte client connection id plus a 32-byte X25519 public key. The client resends with backoff from 0.25 s to 1 s."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_handshake", "label": "HANDSHAKE packet 0x02", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Cleartext server hello: 4-byte server connection id, 32-byte X25519 public key, 32-byte Ed25519 public key, and a 64-byte signature over the transcript."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_data", "label": "DATA packet 0x10", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Application packet. The packet number is 8 bytes and strictly increasing. The nonce is four zero bytes concatenated with that number. Each direction has its own key."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_stream_frame", "label": "STREAM frame", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Types 0x02 and 0x03 (FIN). Fields are stream id (4), offset (8), length (2), and data. FIN ends the stream."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_ack_frame", "label": "ACK SACK frame", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Type 0x04. Fields are largest (8), delay in microseconds (4), range count (1), and up to 6 [start, end] ranges, so only missing spans are resent."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_max_stream_data", "label": "MAX_STREAM_DATA frame", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Type 0x05. Grants more credit (stream id and max offset) to the sender."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_ping", "label": "PING frame", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Type 0x06. Keep-alive every 4 seconds when idle, and the probe when a PTO fires."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_close", "label": "CLOSE frame", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Type 0x07. Closes the connection with a code and a reason, and is sent three times."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_application_messages", "label": "Length-prefixed JSON messages", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Each application message is a uint32 length plus UTF-8 JSON and always has a type field t. Client control stream 0 carries AUTH, LIST, and BYE. Server control stream 1 carries AUTH_OK, AUTH_FAIL, PEERS, SENT, and ERR."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_auth", "label": "AUTH message", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "AUTH {user, pass} on client stream 0. A new name is registered automatically. Passwords are stored with PBKDF2-SHA256, 120000 iterations, and a salt. AUTH_OK returns the user and peers; AUTH_FAIL returns a message."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_msg", "label": "MSG message", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "MSG {id, to, text} where to is a user name or star for broadcast. A new stream with FIN is used. The server adds from and ts and forwards it."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_file", "label": "FILE and FILE_END", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "FILE {id, to, name, size}, then bytes, then FILE_END {sha256}, on a new low-priority stream. The server rebuilds a reliable header and relays cut-through. SENT and ERR acknowledge the relay or report offline users and files that are too large."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_connection_state_machine", "label": "Connection state machine", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The client sends INITIAL and moves through CONNECTING. The server caches the HANDSHAKE reply. After the signature is verified and the key is pinned, both sides derive keys with HKDF and become ESTABLISHED. AUTH then yields ONLINE and a PEERS push, or AUTH_FAIL. Idle beyond 20 seconds or CLOSE moves the user OFFLINE and updates PEERS. An unexpected loss enters RECONNECTING with backoff 1, 2, 4, up to 15 seconds and an automatic login."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_pbkdf2", "label": "PBKDF2-SHA256", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_credit_deadlock", "label": "Relay credit deadlock", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The server withholds the sender's stream credit when the queue toward the receiver grows too large. A real bug: the second credit hold overwrote the previous callback, so credit was never returned and the send stream stalled forever under loss. The fix keeps a list of callbacks and releases all of them when the receiver connection closes. An end-to-end test with packet loss caught it."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_auto_reconnect", "label": "Auto-reconnect and connection migration", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "PING every 4 seconds and a 20 second idle timeout run on both sides. The server drops the user and broadcasts PEERS. The client reconnects with exponential backoff and logs in again. A valid packet from a new address updates the peer address (connection migration). A duplicate login kicks the previous session. This covers unexpected loss and NAT rebinding."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_http_gateway", "label": "Minimal HTTP gateway", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The browser cannot speak UDP, and HTTP frameworks are forbidden, so the gateway is a minimal asyncio TCP HTTP server with a parser, server-sent events, and a streaming upload. Uploads use XHR with Content-Length. The gateway reads 64 KB at a time and awaits drain so back-pressure reaches the browser TCP connection. A custom header, X-Requested-With, plus a Host check, blocks CSRF and DNS rebinding. Path traversal is rejected. Tests cover static files, the API, SSE, streamed POST upload, download, a missing X-Requested-With, a foreign Host, and path traversal."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_test_matrix", "label": "Automated and browser test matrix", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Unit tests cover RangeSet, frame encode and decode, ECDH, AES-GCM rejection of a tampered header, and PBKDF2. Transport tests move 4 MB cleanly, survive 5 percent loss plus reordering under CUBIC and BBR with a matching SHA-256, enforce the flow-control window, reject a server with the wrong key, and notice a disconnect. End-to-end with three clients covers login, presence, broadcast, unicast that does not leak, offline ERR, a 3 MB file, an empty file, a client that vanishes, a duplicate login, and auto-reconnect, including a link with 4 percent loss and 15 ms delay under BBR. Chromium drove two users through chat, a private message, and a 4 MB file whose download matched SHA-256, with zero JavaScript errors. Every listed group passed."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_known_limits", "label": "Known security and scope limits", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "INITIAL has no anti-amplification or address-spoofing cookie and retry token of the kind QUIC uses. BBR is a reduced version 1. The finished-stream list used to stop replay is kept in RAM. Only IPv4 is supported."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_selective_retransmit", "label": "QUIC-style selective retransmit", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "As in TCP, an acknowledgement of a retransmitted packet makes the RTT sample ambiguous. Every transmission uses a fresh packet number, in the QUIC style, and the sender remembers which frames rode in which packet. When a packet is declared lost, only that byte range is requeued. The whole file is not sent again."}, {"id": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_textbook_chapters", "label": "Course textbook chapters 2, 3, 6, and 8", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The design table cites the course textbook: chapters 2, 3, 6, and 8, covering HTTP evolution and QUIC, reliable transfer and congestion control, discovery, and cryptographic primitives. The book title is not printed in the report."}], "edges": [{"source": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_technical_report", "target": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_three_layer_architecture", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_three_layer_architecture", "target": "source_code_readme_common_transport_py", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_three_layer_architecture", "target": "source_code_readme_client_core_py", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "graphify-out/converted/report_337b3c07.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "graphify_out_converted_report_337b3c07_three_layer_architecture", "target": "source_code_readme_server_server_py", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file"